Run the MTP drafter in fp16 without touching its weights.

A bf16-trained checkpoint can overflow fp16 in the residual stream before the
next pre-norm. The drafter's embedding table and residual scale are shifted by a
power of two at load time, which leaves normalized activations alone and does
not change Q/K/V, MLP gate/up, convolution or selector weights. Layer
subclasses with their own initialization keep their original range, and the
embedding table stays shared with the target after loading.

